                     <incipit xml:lang="ar">
                        <locus target="#1v">-1v</locus>وَمَنْ يَقْنُتْ مِنْكُنَّ لِلَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ وَتَعْمَلْ صَالِحًا نُؤْتِهَا أَجْرَهَا مَرَّتَيْنِ وَأَعْتَدْنَا لَهَا رِزْقًا كَرِيمًا<note>33:31</note>
                     </incipit>
                     <explicit xml:lang="ar">
                        <locus target="#42v">-42v</locus>اِتَّبِعُوا مَنْ لَا يَسْئلكُمْ أَجْرًا وَهُمْ مُهْتَدُونَ<note>36:21</note>
                     </explicit>
                     <note>33:31-36:21. Some words are divided into two different lines.
The “ḏāl” is written with the dot inside. Sometimes the catchword is only the first part of the first word in the next page, sometimes there is a repeated word and the catchword is the second word of the next page. 
The name of the scribe appears in mss. 4521D (37r) and 4521X (1v, 45v). The note stating that he is actually the scribe is in 4521X (45v). </note>
                     <note/>
